# ---- Model weights (40 GB) — fetched by fetch-models.sh, never committed ----
# Contents, not the directory itself: git does not descend into an excluded directory, so
# `backend/models/` would make the `!` below unreachable. `backend/models/*` excludes every
# child exactly the same way while leaving the parent visible enough to re-include one file.
backend/models/*
# The committed files under models/. Neither is a weight; both are the record of what the
# weights are, and the rule above is about 40 GB of fetched binaries.
#
# PROVENANCE.json records which upstream artefact each house-named weight came from, which
# is the only place that mapping exists -- the weights themselves are fetched, so if the
# manifest is not in git it is not anywhere.
!backend/models/PROVENANCE.json
# NAMES.md is the same mapping written for a person rather than for fetch-models.sh, and it
# is where attribution is rendered from. Apache-2.0 and MIT let us rename what we
# redistribute and do not let us drop the notice, so this file is a licence obligation, not
# documentation -- untracking it is the one way to lose the notice without deleting it.
!backend/models/NAMES.md

# ---- Vendored third-party checkouts (each carries its own .git) ----
cuda-samples/
# NVIDIA/skills
# ---- Virtualenvs ----
training/.venv/
**/.venv/
# One venv per app, named symoneural-<extension>.venv after the app it belongs to (Garrett's
# rule, 2026-08-20, the same rule as the per-app env files). This line is not decoration:
# `**/.venv/` above matches on the literal name `.venv`, so a directory called
# `symoneural-discovery.venv` is NOT covered by it and a venv full of compiled wheels would
# be committable. The pattern had to widen in the same change that introduced the new name.
**/symoneural-*.venv/

# ---- Runtime output: logs, screenshots, generated images ----
run/
backend/output/

# ---- Vendored Swagger UI assets ----
site/swagger-ui-bundle.js
site/swagger-ui.css

# ---- Junk ----
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.bak
*.log

# ---- Trained adapter weights (244 MB) — artifacts, not source ----
*.safetensors
*.gguf
training/adapter/tokenizer.json
training/adapter-qwen36/tokenizer.json

# ---- Nested third-party checkouts (belt and braces: these carry their own .git) ----
/cuda-samples
# ---- Reference trees added for the IPTV and Miner surfaces (third-party, large) ----
# Projects_Crypto alone is ~2.5 GB of Antminer/Braiins firmware images.
/cuda-programming-guide.pdf
__MACOSX/

# Vendored design reference for the image page restyle

# ---- Third-party engines we build and ship (contrib) vs read-only material ----
# contrib/ holds source we compile and run; external/ holds material we only read.
/contrib

# ---- external/: reference material, all third-party, consolidated under one directory ----
# Renamed from references/ on 2026-09-08 at the owner's instruction. Same contract:
# read-only, nothing in it may be imported, built, linked or executed. Enforced by
# backend/apps/test_reference_isolation.py, not just written down here.
# Everything here is somebody else's code or a saved document kept for reference.
# ~8.5 GB, none of it ours, none of it tracked.
/external

# ---- Browser "Save page as" captures ----
# site/ is a staging directory that deploy.sh publishes wholesale. An 11.6 MB .mhtml and
# its .pdf print were saved into it to show a broken deploy, and a `git add -A` put both in
# a commit. deploy.sh now filters by extension; this keeps them out of the repo as well.
site/*.mhtml
site/*.pdf

# the snapshots moved out of site/ -- keep them on disk, not in the repo
docs/archive/site-snapshot-*/

# ---- Agent worktrees ----
# Claude Code creates isolated git worktrees under here. Four of them held 105 MB across
# 1,407 files, and each is a checkout of this same repo -- so a `git add -A` from the root
# would try to commit a second copy of the tree into itself. Remove a stale one with
# `git worktree remove --force .claude/worktrees/<name>`, never with rm -rf, or git keeps
# the registration and `git worktree list` lies to the next agent.
.claude/worktrees/

# ---- Session scratch ----
# Neither of these is source. q.json is a hand-built request body for probing the tuner
# guide; claudesessioninterrupted is a partial tool call captured when a session died.
/q.json
.claude/claudesessioninterrupted

# Contract signing private keys. The public half (contract-signing.pub) ships and is
# tracked; the private half must never be committed, never be on a rig, and never be in
# the environment of a web process. Belt and braces -- issue.py also chmods it 0600.
*.key
!contrib/**/*.key
backend/apps/licensing/contract-signing.key

# ---- Agent scratch, not source ----
# .claude/claudesessioninterrupted is a 271 KB dump of one interrupted session's transcript,
# written by the harness. HANDOVER.md is the state document by house rule ("do not create a
# second state document"); a raw transcript beside it is a second one that nobody maintains.
.claude/claudesessioninterrupted

# q.json is a scratch request body left at the repo root from hand-testing the tuner's guide
# endpoint. Nothing reads it -- a grep across the tree finds no reference.
/q.json

# ---- Live agent worktrees ----
# .claude/worktrees/ holds real `git worktree` checkouts (138 MB, four of them as this was
# written), each on its own branch with its own agent working inside it. Committing them
# into the main tree would nest a checkout inside its own repository -- and they are not
# source in any case, they are other branches' working copies. `git worktree list` is how
# you enumerate them; `git worktree remove` is how they go away.
.claude/worktrees/

# Rust build output for symon-gateway. Hundreds of megabytes of compiled dependencies, and
# `cargo build` reproduces all of it from Cargo.toml + Cargo.lock. Cargo.lock IS tracked --
# it is a binary that gets deployed, so the exact dependency versions matter.
backend/apps/gateway/target/

# The compiled Go validator. Built by `make build-wasm`; see ops/middleware/limiter.go.
ops/middleware/*.wasm

# ---- the render UI ----
# Garrett dropped an upstream clone at the repo root and this rule excluded it by name. It is
# not there any more: the 2026-08 restructure swept it into external/, and on 2026-08-21
# `tools/fork_render_ui.sh` forked it to `engines/symoneural-ui` -- which the `# /engines/ — the directory is GONE as of 2026-09-08. The five runtimes moved to the
# repo root and the compatibility symlinks were deleted once the live API was proved to
# be running the fixed paths. The rule is kept commented rather than removed so that a
# stray engines/ reappearing is noticed rather than silently ignored.
# /engines/` rule
# further down already covers, so THIS pattern now excludes nothing and is kept only for the
# paragraph below. Do not "tidy" the name back to something that matches: a rule matching a
# literal directory name stops matching the moment the directory is renamed, and 8.4 GB of
# untracked venv becomes stageable by the next `git add -A`. `/engines/` is the live rule.
#
# Why it must stay excluded, unchanged by the move: 8.4 GB with its venv and its frontend
# package, and it is GPL-3.0, so tracking it would put a copyleft tree inside a repo whose
# LICENSES.md says nothing shipped is copyleft. `extra_model_paths.yaml` points it at
# backend/models/image/, so no weights are copied and nothing in there is a second source of
# truth for them.
#
# The one thing in that tree that is OURS is the custom node `symoneural_gguf` -- the
# DualCLIPLoaderSDCPP that accepts this box's diffusion-engine T5 file, which upstream's
# loader rejects outright. It cannot be rescued with a `!` negation here: git does not descend
# into an excluded directory, so no pattern can re-include a file underneath one. (That was
# tried; `git check-ignore -v` still reported the exclusion, which is the correct behaviour and
# not a typo.) The source of truth therefore lives in the repo at
# backend/apps/studio/custom_nodes/symoneural_gguf/, and scripts/symoneural-ui.sh links it
# into place on start. Delete engines/symoneural-ui and re-fork it and nothing of ours is lost.
/ComfyUI/

# ---- symoneural-discovery credentials ----
# One env file per app, named symoneural-<extension>.env after the app. The tracked
# template is symoneural-discovery.env.example with every value empty; the real file is
# the line below. It is listed BEFORE the file exists on purpose: backend/apps/remix/.env is
# tracked with live secret-shaped values and rides inside any archive carrying .git, and the
# only reason that happened is that nothing excluded it until after the first `git add -A`.
backend/apps/studio/plugins/discovery/symoneural-discovery.env

# Real env files never travel; the .example files do.
.env
*.env
!*.env.example

# Review artefacts: uncommitted by standing rule, same as the root ones they hold.
/review/
/AUDIT.md

# Rust build output for the gateway. 7,620 files were staged in one `git add -A` on
# 2026-08-20 because this was never ignored; cargo regenerates all of it from Cargo.toml.
backend/apps/gateway/target/
# The desktop client's Rust build output -- 2.4 GB on a debug profile, and every
# byte of it regenerable by `cargo build`. Listed explicitly like the other two
# target/ directories rather than as a bare `target/`, which would also swallow any
# directory a future app happens to call that.
desktop-client/src-tauri/target/
# tauri-build regenerates these four schema files on every compile from the permissions of
# whatever crates are in the dependency tree. 4,600 lines that describe the toolchain rather
# than this program, and they would churn on any Tauri version bump. The one hand-written
# file in that tree, capabilities/default.json, is NOT here and is tracked -- it is a grant,
# and a grant that changed without a diff would be the thing you most wanted to see.
desktop-client/src-tauri/gen/

# Mode 600 and private, and they must stay untracked. Both were staged by a `git add -A` on
# 2026-08-20 and caught before the commit; an ignore rule is the durable version of catching it.
INTAKE.md
GARRETT-REQUESTS.md

# Built engines. engines/ is produced by tools/fork_engines.sh from the vendored upstream
# source in external/ (itself untracked), so it is a derived tree: 874 MB of C++ plus a
# ~1.1 GB build. The tracked artefact is the script, which is the provenance -- re-run it
# and you get the same engines. Do not track a derived copy of an untracked source.
/engines/

# Third-party application binaries Garrett drops in for reference. `bin/` is otherwise
# tracked, and these are large (claude is ~308 MB) and proprietary -- committing one
# would put somebody else's licensed application into this history permanently, and
# there is no remote to make that recoverable by deleting it upstream.
bin/claude
bin/claude-desktop

# Browser-test harness. `tests/package.json` is the tracked record of what is pinned and
# `npm install` in tests/ reproduces it; 13 MB of node_modules in git is not provenance,
# the version pin is. Playwright never ships -- see backend/DEPENDENCIES.md.
tests/node_modules/

# Debian build products of yocto/oematch. `dpkg-buildpackage` writes them one directory up
# from the source, which is `yocto/`, not a build directory -- so they land in the repo and
# read as new source files. The .deb is 60 kB of the tree beside it and goes stale the moment
# anything under yocto/oematch/ changes; rebuild it rather than tracking it.
yocto/oematch_*.deb
yocto/oematch_*.buildinfo
yocto/oematch_*.changes

# Adopted reference projects, staged for rebranding by `tools/index_references.py adopt`.
# The provenance record is tracked; the copied tree is not -- `external/` is the
# original and re-adopting is one command, so committing a second copy of somebody
# else's tree buys nothing and quietly puts it under our history.
# git will not re-include a file whose parent directory is excluded, so the directory
# has to be un-excluded first and its contents excluded separately. Written out because
# the obvious two-line form silently ignores the negation.
incoming/*
!incoming/*/
incoming/*/*
!incoming/*/PROVENANCE.md

# Local archive artefacts, written by the archive procedure in
# docs/share-previews/ARCHIVE-PLAN.md. They are built FROM committed state, so tracking one
# stores a second copy of history inside the history it was cut from -- and the next archive
# would then contain the previous one, compounding on every run.
#
# The manifests are ignored with them, deliberately. A manifest names the SHA of the archive
# it describes, so committing it changes the tree, which changes the next commit, which is a
# document that is wrong the moment it is right. The durable record of a stage is the git
# tag; the tarball and its manifest are local artefacts of a moment.
releases/

# ---- symoneural-web build output ----
# The BINARY is not committed; `ops/build-symoneural-web.sh`, main.go, go.mod and go.sum are,
# and those four reproduce it byte-for-byte (proved: two clean builds with -trimpath produced
# an identical digest). A targeted ignore rather than ignoring the whole component, so the
# generated SBOM and build manifest beside it stay reviewable in a diff.
web/symoneural-web/build/symoneural-web

# ---- Consolidated home-directory bulk: archives, backups and weight staging ----
# 2026-09-08. Twenty-six loose directories under /home/google were reviewed and brought
# under this repo so there is one tree to back up instead of a scattered home directory.
# They split three ways, and only the third is tracked:
#
#   external/     somebody else's code and saved documents, read-only. Already ignored
#                 above. The consolidation added oss/ (46 upstream trees), TensorRT-LLM,
#                 ComfyUI-Installs, ComfyUI-Shared, bentoml, ninjatraining, Toolchains,
#                 Platforms and the vendor agent material.
#   archive/      this rule. Bulk that is not reference material and not source: 253 GB of
#                 workspace archives, 16 GB of backups, build outputs, and model/LoRA
#                 weight staging. Nothing here is ours to relicense and nothing here is
#                 read by the running system.
#   docs/         the small first-party part -- decision records, reconciliation notes,
#                 restoration manifests, host diagnostics. Tracked, and committed one
#                 directory per commit so a mistake is attributable to a single item.
#
# The moves were same-filesystem renames, so they cost no disk: 618 GB used before and
# 618 GB after. Tracking archive/ would double 273 GB into .git objects and would put
# model weights into history permanently, which docs/ops/EXPORTS.md rule 2 forbids.
/archive

# ---- The five first-party runtimes, promoted to top level 2026-09-08 ----------------
# Owner instruction: each runtime gets its own folder at the repo root --
# symoneural-llm/, symoneural-diffuse/, symoneural-listen/, symoneural-miner/,
# symoneural-ui/ -- rather than living under engines/.
#
# Ignored, not tracked, and that was the explicit decision. These are DERIVED trees: 11 GB
# of C++ and CUDA objects produced by tools/fork_engines.sh from vendored upstream source.
# Rule /engines/ above already said "do not track a derived copy of an untracked source"
# and the reasoning does not change because the directory moved up a level. What is tracked
# is the recipe that rebuilds them and the notices that say what they are.
#
# The moment these appeared at the root they became five untracked directories that the
# next `git add -A` would have swept into a commit. That is the accident this rule exists
# to prevent, and it is why the rule landed in the same session as the move rather than
# after it.
#
# engines/ still exists and now holds five compatibility symlinks pointing here, so the
# running api.py -- which resolves engine paths at spawn time and has not been restarted --
# keeps working. The shims come out once every path reference is updated and the API has
# been restarted deliberately.
/symoneural-llm/
/symoneural-diffuse/
/symoneural-listen/
/symoneural-miner/
/symoneural-ui/

# symoneural-code/ and symoneural-audit/ are deliberately NOT listed above. They are
# runtime folders like the five, but they are OURS -- first-party source, not a derived
# tree built from somebody else's release tarball -- so a clone carries them. That is the
# whole distinction: the five are rebuilt by tools/fork_engines.sh and would be 11 GB of
# committed objects; these two are what we wrote.

# ---- Retired provider-specific authority paths, 2026-09-08 -------------------------
# The rules moved to docs/authority/PROJECT-AUTHORITY.md, the handoff to docs/HANDOFF.md,
# and the deny rules to docs/authority/TOOL-POLICY.json, which a test enforces. These
# paths are untracked rather than deleted: the physical copies are still needed for the
# owner's retirement backup, and ignoring them means that later physical purge cannot
# dirty a finished repository.
/.claude/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Private recovery state — bare-metal reset, 2026-09-08.
#
# `.private/` holds the things Git cannot restore: consistent copies of live
# application databases, the installed host configuration, secret runtime
# config, the Claude retirement backups, generated source bundles and verified
# build artefacts. It is recovery state, NOT source. Git remains the source
# authority; this tree exists so a fresh Linux install can be brought back to
# a running system, and it travels only inside the owner's verified archive.
#
# It is ignored before it is populated, deliberately: the reverse order puts
# secrets and multi-gigabyte databases in front of `git add -A`, and this repo
# has already had two mode-600 ledgers staged by exactly that accident.
/.private/
